pproval/_held_row_line/_held_act_lines/held_reattest_refusal/reattest_scope_refusal\"","-detail = \"acceptance_record.merge_approval_refusal applies the additional adjudication bounds whenever any claimed row records a scope, including a mixed lane, and calls reattest_scope_refusal for that scope-bound lane and held_reattest_refusal for every lane. A malformed combined token is refused by name; an act-free delta has no scope refusal. reattested_between reads the ledger at the merge base and the head and takes the ids re-attested by the entries whose seq the base does not hold, refusing by name when either side does not parse. Every such id outside amendment_scope - the union of the claimed amendment rows' own amendment scope, including their amendment:<id> tokens when a combined sitting claims them, empty when none is claimed - is refused by name before the first-approval judgement runs. For each re-attested row whose tier the dial at trunk's tip holds - trunk's tip being the commit the merge lands on, not the merge base - held_reattest_refusal refuses the row by name when it is below approval or absent at the head, when its act names no verdict, when its named verdict is not accepted - bound, recorded accepted and valid - or when that accepted verdict's parsed amendment row rulings do not rule the row CLARITY; a row tagged both MEANING and CLARITY reads MEANING. Every approval act in the lane’s complete acted set - a live flip or born-approved row, a ledger-only approval, or a re-attestation - is refused when any row it approves lacks an APPROVE ruling in an accepted first-approval part or any row it re-attests lacks a MEANING or CLARITY ruling in an accepted amendment part among the branch's bindings, whether or not the act names a verdict; a named verdict must also judge every row its act re-attests, and acts already on trunk are not re-read. An act that both approves rows and re-attests rows, claimed either by two single-kind rows or by one combined sitting's per-kind tokens, merges when each half lies inside its claiming row's own kind scope, whether their rows are in one registry or different ones; an id under another combined kind authorizes neither half, a snapshot is WIDENED only when the complete acted set holds no approved or re-attested row in that registry identity: every carrier path of an authorized registry, including a deleted obsolete carrier copy, is authorized, while an unauthorized registry's copy is WIDENED on every carrier path, and every held re-attestation has its CLARITY verdict.\"","+code_symbol = \"first_approval_scope/adjudication_approval_refusal/merge_approval_refusal/_registry_identity/lane_acted_set/_copy_lines/amendment_scope/reattested_between/_wrote_ledger/_added_acts/row_rulings/verdict_rulings/_tier_of/_below_approval/_held_row_line/_held_act_lines/held_reattest_refusal/reattest_scope_refusal\"","+detail = \"acceptance_record.merge_approval_refusal applies the additional adjudication bounds whenever any claimed row records a scope, including a mixed lane, and calls reattest_scope_refusal for that scope-bound lane and held_reattest_refusal for every lane. A malformed combined token is refused by name; an act-free delta has no scope refusal. reattested_between reads the ledger at the merge base and the head and takes the ids re-attested by the entries whose seq the base does not hold, refusing by name when either side does not parse. Every such id outside amendment_scope - the union of the claimed amendment rows' own amendment scope, including their amendment:<id> tokens when a combined sitting claims them, empty when none is claimed - is refused by name before the first-approval judgement runs. For each re-attested row whose tier the dial at trunk's tip holds - trunk's tip being the commit the merge lands on, not the merge base - held_reattest_refusal refuses the row by name when it is below approval or absent at the head, when its act names no verdict, when its named verdict is not accepted - bound, recorded accepted and valid - or when that accepted verdict's parsed amendment row rulings do not rule the row CLARITY; a row tagged both MEANING and CLARITY reads MEANING. Every approval act in the lane’s complete acted set - a live flip or born-approved row, a ledger-only approval, or a re-attestation - is refused when any row it approves lacks an APPROVE ruling in an accepted first-approval part or any row it re-attests lacks a MEANING or CLARITY ruling in an accepted amendment part among the branch's bindings, whether or not the act names a verdict; a named verdict must also judge every row its act re-attests, and acts already on trunk are not re-read. An act that both approves rows and re-attests rows, claimed either by two single-kind rows or by one combined sitting's per-kind tokens, merges when each half lies inside its claiming row's own kind scope, whether their rows are in one registry or different ones; an id under another combined kind authorizes neither half, a snapshot is WIDENED only when the complete acted set holds no approved or re-attested row in that registry identity, a row the act ledger names being placed in the registry its id's tier names whether or not the row remains in the live registry at the head, so a retirement authorizes its own registry's copy: every carrier path of an authorized registry, including a deleted obsolete carrier copy, is authorized, while an unauthorized registry's copy is WIDENED on every carrier path, and every held re-attestation has its CLARITY verdict.\""," test_refs = \"TC-278; TC-331\""," rationale = \"\"\"At merge the first-approval scope check read flips alone, and a re-attestation moves no cell, so an amendment act could re-anchor rows its verdict never ruled. The act ledger is the one record naming the rows each act re-attested, which makes the scope checkable where the merge is judged; a scope-bound lane claiming no amendment has ruled no amended text, so it may re-anchor none.\"\"\""," status = \"Approved\""]}]},{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-942\\docs\\archive\\last_approved\\docs\\test\\test-cases.toml","hunks":[{"oldStart":2827,"oldLines":11,"newStart":2827,"newLines":11,"lines":[" [test.TC-278]"," verifies = [\"SR-178\", \"SR-228\", \"LLR-278\", \"IF-091\"]"," level = \"Integration\"","-method = \"Driven on real git repositories made from scaffolds. An adjudication lane claiming an amendment row scoped to one requirement, whose act re-attests two amended requirements, is refused at merge naming the one outside the scope; an act re-attesting only the scoped row merges, and the same act claimed by a first-approval row is refused naming the row, since that lane holds no re-attestation scope. The held authority is read at trunk's tip rather than the merge base. A combined act across the same or different registries merges when each section acts only within its own kind scope and every copied registry has an acted row, including a ledger-only approval; a copied registry neither section moved is WIDENED. With the rung held, a re-attestation without a verdict, of a row below approval, with a MEANING verdict, with a verdict that does not rule the row, with a named verdict file absent at the act's head, with an accepted verdict that is invalid as a whole, or with a verdict tagging the row both MEANING and CLARITY is refused by row, while an accepted verdict ruling an approved row CLARITY merges. On a RELEASED rung, an act naming an unaccepted verdict is refused by the verdict named, and its whole act, a first approval included, is refused. Each act permitted to merge in these cases carries branch bindings that judge every row it flips APPROVE and every row it re-attests MEANING or CLARITY, written by _bind_branch; a named verdict must itself be accepted and judge each row its act re-attests, and omitting --verdict does not waive the branch binding. A first-approval part that RETURNs its only judged row approves nothing, while its combined act may re-attest its amendment row. One act that approves a Drafted requirement and re-attests an amended one, claimed by a first-approval row scoped to the first and an amendment row scoped to the second, merges; the same act whose amendment row is scoped to another requirement is refused naming the re-attested row.\"","+method = \"Driven on real git repositories made from scaffolds. An adjudication lane claiming an amendment row scoped to one requirement, whose act re-attests two amended requirements, is refused at merge naming the one outside the scope; an act re-attesting only the scoped row merges, and the same act claimed by a first-approval row is refused naming the row, since that lane holds no re-attestation scope. The held authority is read at trunk's tip rather than the merge base. A combined act across the same or different registries merges when each section acts only within its own kind scope and every copied registry has an acted row, including a ledger-only approval; a copied registry neither section moved is WIDENED. With the rung held, a re-attestation without a verdict, of a row below approval, with a MEANING verdict, with a verdict that does not rule the row, with a named verdict file absent at the act's head, with an accepted verdict that is invalid as a whole, or with a verdict tagging the row both MEANING and CLARITY is refused by row, while an accepted verdict ruling an approved row CLARITY merges. On a RELEASED rung, an act naming an unaccepted verdict is refused by the verdict named, and its whole act, a first approval included, is refused. Each act permitted to merge in these cases carries branch bindings that judge every row it flips APPROVE and every row it re-attests MEANING or CLARITY, written by _bind_branch; a named verdict must itself be accepted and judge each row its act re-attests, and omitting --verdict does not waive the branch binding. A first-approval part that RETURNs its only judged row approves nothing, while its combined act may re-attest its amendment row. One act that approves a Drafted requirement and re-attests an amended one, claimed by a first-approval row scoped to the first and an amendment row scoped to the second, merges; the same act whose amendment row is scoped to another requirement is refused naming the re-attested row. On a RELEASED rung, an act whose only re-attestation retires an Approved row the lane deleted, ruled by an accepted amendment verdict inside the amendment scope, merges with that registry's copy as its only copy. The same retirement on a held rung is refused by row.\""," tier = \"Full\"","-expected = \"Satisfies LLR-278 (parents SR-178 and SR-228): a re-attestation outside the amendment row's Adjudicates scope is refused at merge by name; on a held rung only an accepted verdict ruling the row CLARITY permits it; every acted approval, including a ledger-only approval, needs its approved rows APPROVE-ruled and its re-attested rows MEANING- or CLARITY-ruled by accepted branch bindings, and a named verdict judges each row it re-attests; and an act whose approvals and re-attestations each lie in the scope of the row claiming them merges.\"","+expected = \"Satisfies LLR-278 (parents SR-178 and SR-228): a re-attestation outside the amendment row's Adjudicates scope is refused at merge by name; on a held rung only an accepted verdict ruling the row CLARITY permits it; every acted approval, including a ledger-only approval, needs its approved rows APPROVE-ruled and its re-attested rows MEANING- or CLARITY-ruled by accepted branch bindings, and a named verdict judges each row it re-attests; and an act whose approvals and re-attestations each lie in the scope of the row claiming them merges. A retired row's re-attestation authorizes its own registry's copy, whether or not another act moved that registry.\""," automated = \"Yes\"","-evidence = \"tests/test_snapshot_readers.py::test_a_reattestation_outside_the_amendment_scope_is_refused_by_name; tests/test_snapshot_read